 - Bash: cd /tmp && head -2 blame.txt | cat -A | head -2; wc -l active.txt; grep -c . blame.txt; grep -F -f active.txt blame.txt
 - Bash: D=$(mktemp -d) && git ls-files '*.go' | grep -v '^vendor/' | while read f; do git blame -w -M -C --line-porcelain HEAD -- "$f" 2>/dev/null | grep -a '^author-mail ' | sed 's/author-mail <\(.*\)>/\1/'; done | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn > $D/b.txt
git log --since='2025-04-08' --no-merges --format='%ae' | sort -u > $D/a.txt
awk 'NR==FNR{a[tolower($1)]=1;next}{t+=$1; if(a[tolower($2)]){s+=$1; print $1,$2}} END{print "active",s,"of",t}' $D/a.txt $D/b.txt
echo; head -12 $D/b.txt; awk '{t+=$1;c+=$1; if(NR==1||NR==2||NR==3||NR==5||NR==10)print NR, c, c/12682}' $D/b.txt
FINAL ANSWER:
**Short answer:** yes, it's a problem. The bigger problem is that most of the code belongs to people who have left, not that a few current people hold it.

**What I measured.** I ran `git blame` (ignoring whitespace, following moves) over the 76 non-vendored Go files. That gave 12,682 surviving lines. "Active" means committed in the 12 months before the newest commit (2026-04-08). The history is 354 commits from 2012 to 2026.

**Who wrote the live code**

| Author | Live lines | Share | Active? |
|---|---|---|---|
| <email redacted> | 2,020 | 15.9% | no, last commit 2018 |
| alex.ogier | 1,237 | 9.8% | no, last commit 2015 |
| noahdietz | 1,228 | 9.7% | no, last commit 2018 |
| JReyLBC | 1,044 | 8.2% | no, last commit 2017 |
| tamal@appscode, riobard, mitchconnors, rpothier, and others | each 380–620 | | no |
| **sam.okafor** | 537 | 4.2% | yes |
| **georges.varouchas** | 454 | 3.6% | yes |
| **eth-p** | 295 | 2.3% | yes |
| mfrank (mercari), hujun.work | 172, 136 | 1.4%, 1.1% | yes |
| lena.voss, patrick.ohly, tomasaschan | 133, 108, 67 | about 1% each | yes |

- **Orphaned code:** only about 2,000 of 12,682 lines (15.9%) are written by anyone active. Roughly 84% of the Go code has no active author. The top four authors by surviving lines are all inactive, and together they wrote about 43% of it.
- **Concentration among active people:** it is high. sam.okafor, georges.varouchas and eth-p account for about 1,290 of the 2,012 active lines (64%). The tool's fallback owner rule names sam.okafor, tomasaschan and georges.varouchas.
- **If you lose one or two people:** losing sam.okafor and georges.varouchas would remove about half of the active knowledge. Nobody active has a deep line count anywhere. No active author owns more than 4.2% of the code.

**What this means for reviewing**
- You will often be reviewing code that nobody currently working on the project wrote. Those areas are orphaned, not just low bus factor.
- Tell the people who do know the code (the three above) about this, and spread their knowledge before one of them leaves.
- I did not get per-directory results. The tool's dry run printed only the fallback rule, and I didn't run it in write mode because that would add files to your clean repo. I can run it in write mode if you want per-directory orphaned and bus-factor-1 zones in `.github/ownership.json`.

**Caveats**
- Line counts are a proxy for knowledge. A refactor or a gofmt-style sweep can inflate someone's share. The latest commit, an `interface{}` to `any` rewrite, is one example.
- "Active" is measured against 2026-04-08, the newest commit, not today (2026-10-22). Someone who has been quiet since then, or a reviewer who never commits, would be misclassified.
- I didn't merge duplicate emails for the same person. I used raw blame emails, so some people may be split across two identities.
- I ran with `--no-github`, so the output shows bare emails and no GitHub handles.

