FINAL ANSWER:
Surviving Go code is spread across many authors. Nobody who wrote much of it is still active, so the risk is that no one else knows the code, not that you lose one of the top two.

**Method:** `git blame -w -M -C` on all 76 non-vendor Go files at HEAD. I ignored the `interface{}` to `any` commit (6bf5b04), which would otherwise have taken credit for lines it only reformatted. I excluded `vendor/` and `zz_generated_flags.go`. That leaves 12,194 live lines.

| Author | Live lines | Share | Active |
|---|---|---|---|
| Eric Paris | 2041 | 16.7% | 2015–2018 (103 commits) |
| Alex Ogier | 1237 | 10.1% | 2012–2015 (37 commits) |
| Noah Dietz | 1232 | 10.1% | 2018 (2 commits) |
| Jonathan Reyna | 1047 | 8.6% | 2017 (1 commit) |
| Tamal Saha | 626 | 5.1% | 2018 (2 commits) |
| Riobard | 538 | 4.4% | 2014 (1 commit) |
| Mitch Connors | 522 | 4.3% | 2019 (9 commits) |
| Georges Varouchas | 454 | 3.7% | 2025 (4 commits) |
| Rest (about 100 more) | 4,497 | 37% | mixed |

- **Concentration:** The top two authors hold 26.9% of the code, and the top four hold 45.6%. That is low for a library this old. Losing one or two people would not leave you without knowledge of most of the code.
- **Who is still around:** Almost all the top authors left years ago. In the last 12 months only Sam Okafor (3 commits, last on 2026-04-08) and Lena Voss (1 commit, 2026-03-05) committed, plus dependabot. Lena Voss owns 143 live lines. Sam Okafor owns 488 lines of `zz_generated_flags.go`, but I did not count the hand-written lines Sam owns. They fall below the 25-author cutoff I printed (about 106 lines or fewer).
- **Practical bus factor:** If "the people" means current committers, the bus factor is about 1 to 2, and Sam Okafor is the one to keep. Eric Paris is the best historical reference, with 103 commits and the most live code, but there has been no activity since 2018.
- **Large single-commit authors:** Noah Dietz, Jonathan Reyna and Riobard each own 500 to 1,200 lines from one or two commits. Those are probably large feature or test drops, and nobody has touched them since. Check `git log --author` for those commits before you review PRs that touch that code.

I did not check whether the people who wrote the code are the same ones who understand it now. Also, three alias pairs in the shortlog (bogem, therealmitchconnors, Albert Nigmatzianov) share emails with their main identity, so the table already counts them together.

I can break this down per file to show which files have a single author. Say so if you want it.
